{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789240044,"nonce":"v6MR6hIKVWmGRloImp98LLWjOLo=","title":"Module global variables in the SSA builder and backends","message":"A `TypedDeclaration::Variable` lowered to a `HirGlobal` had no reader or\nwriter: the SSA builder resolved a `Variable` to a local, a function\nreference or a constructor, never to a module global, so no frontend\ncould give a program module-level state (Python's module variables and\n`global` statement need it).\n\nNow the builder takes the module's globals by name and type\n(`with_module_globals`), reads one with a Load through a\n`HirValueKind::Global` address and writes one with a Store, unless a\n`let` or parameter in the function binds the same name. Globals are\nlowered before any body, like effects. Cranelift declares a mutable\nglobal writable (it declared every non-vtable global read-only), and\nthe interpreter gives each global one zeroed slot shared across\nfunctions instead of resolving only string constants.\n\nOpen: an initializer that is not a constant is still rejected by\n`lower_global_variable`; the Python frontend emits none and assigns in\nthe entry function instead.","files":null}]}